\f0\b \cf0 $N = 30$ is the universally accepted standard benchmark where you are allowed to invoke the Central Limit
\f1\b0 \
  
\f0\b Theorem (CLT)
\f1\b0  in practical statistics, academic research, and peer review.\
\
  However, the mathematical reality of what the "minimum" actually is depends on the shape of your data:\
\
  ---\
\
  
\f0\b 1. Where Does the "30" Rule Come From?
\f1\b0 \
\
  There is no magical law of nature that switches on at 30. The number 30 comes from 
\f0\b Student\'92s t-distribution
\f1\b0 \
  
\f0\b converging into the standard Normal (Z) curve
\f1\b0 :\
\
   * At $N < 30$, the t-distribution has fat tails to account for uncertainty in your sample standard deviation.\
   * At $N = 30$ (degrees of freedom $df = 29$):\
       * Critical t-value for 95% confidence: 
\f0\b $2.045$
\f1\b0 \
       * Critical Z-value (normal bell curve): 
\f0\b $1.960$
\f1\b0 \
   * Because 2.045 \uc0\u8776  1.96 (less than a 5% difference), statisticians agreed that $N = 30$ is the threshold where the\
     sample mean is close enough to a true Gaussian bell curve to run standard tests without worrying about the\
     underlying population shape.\
\
  ---\
\
  
\f0\b 2. What Is the Real Minimum? (It Depends on the Data)
\f1\b0 \
\
  The Central Limit Theorem states that as N grows, the distribution of the 
\f0\b sample mean
\f1\b0  becomes a bell curve, even if\
  the raw data is not normal. How fast that happens depends on skewness:\
\
  \uc0\u9484 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9516 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9516 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9488 \
