  ---\
\
  
\f0\b 3. How to Apply This to Your 30-Person Study
\f1\b0 \
\
  In human biometrics and financial stress research, follow this standard playbook:\
\
   1. 
\f0\b For EEG & Wearable Telemetry (Muse & Apple Watch):
\f1\b0 \
       * Raw EEG band power (\\muV\'b2) and HRV (RMSSD in ms) are naturally right-skewed.\
       * 
\f0\b The Standard Neuro Practice:
\f1\b0  Take the natural log:\
          Log-Power = ln(Alpha Power),    lnRMSSD = ln(RMSSD)\
       * Once log-transformed, the data becomes almost perfectly symmetric. At 
\f0\b $N = 30$
\f1\b0 , the Central Limit Theorem\
         applies effortlessly, and your t-tests and linear regressions are bulletproof.\
   2. 
\f0\b For Financial Dollar Amounts (Bank Ledgers / Bills):
\f1\b0 \
       * Do not compare raw dollar amounts directly (e.g. \\150$ vs $$3,500$ rent skew the mean).\
       * Instead, measure 
\f0\b percentages
\f1\b0  (e.g., 
\f2\i "Bill outflow as a % of monthly income"
\f1\i0 ) or use the non-parametric\
         Wilcoxon test already established in your research papers.\
\
  
\f0\b Summary
\f1\b0 \
   * 
\f0\b Textbook & Peer-Review Rule:
\f1\b0  
\f0\b $N = 30$
\f1\b0  is the standard number where journals and advisors accept CLT and parametric\
     normality.\
   * 
\f0\b Below 30 ($N < 30$):
\f1\b0  You should use Student\'92s t-tests or non-parametric tests (Mann-Whitney / Wilcoxon /\
     Permutation).\
   * 
\f0\b At 30 ($N = 30$):
\f1\b0  You are officially in the clear to use bell-curve statistics, confidence intervals, and standard\
     projections.\
