\f0\b 3. The Statistical Catch: Why the Basic Central Limit Theorem Doesn't Apply
\f1\b0 \
\
  Here is why you must be careful with academic reviewers:\
\
  The textbook Central Limit Theorem assumes that every observation is 
\f0\b Independent and Identically Distributed
\f1\b0 \
  
\f0\b (i.i.d.)
\f1\b0 :\
   * If you pick 30 random strangers on the street, Person #1\'92s brain has no connection to Person #2\'92s brain. They are\
     independent.\
   * 
\f0\b Your Day 5 is NOT independent of your Day 4:
\f1\b0  If you slept poorly on Tuesday, your brain on Wednesday will still be\
     tired. This is called 
\f0\b Autocorrelation
\f1\b0  (or Serial Correlation).\
\
  Because your days are correlated with each other, you cannot run a simple textbook Student's t-test and claim: 
\f2\i "This
\f1\i0 \
  
\f2\i proves how the human species behaves."
\f1\i0 \
\
